## 1. Introduction
As one of the advanced oxidation processes (AOPs), electrocatalytic oxidation technology has high efficiency in organic wastewater treatment [1]. The rate-determining step of electrocatalytic oxidation is apparently the abundant oxidant generation at the electrode surface [2]. In wide-range scales, these potent oxidants have been examined to degrade a variety of organic compounds. Anode materials play a crucial role to influence the efficiency and energy consumption through the generation of potent oxidants in electrocatalytic oxidation [3]. Therefore, the appropriate selection of the promising anode material is a top priority to improve the efficiency of electrocatalytic oxidation.
Among the well-established multifunctional materials, twodimensional (2D) catalysts with outstanding characteristics (e.g., electron transfer and large field effect) are commonly applied [4]. 2D conductive metal–organic frameworks (C-MOFs) can be established to satisfy diverse applications by altering central metal ions and organic ligands. Among C-MOFs, $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}\mathrm{HITP}_{2}$ is the most favorable for the electrocatalytic oxidation due to high conductivity reaching $5000\ 5{\cdot}{\mathrm{cm}}^{-1}$ [5]. In addition, the periodic appearance of Ni center in $\mathsf{N i}_{3}\mathrm{HITP}_{2}$ is often considered as singlet atomic active sites for the catalytic reaction. The $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking and $\mathtt{d}\mathtt{-}\pi$ conjugate structures in the ligand and Ni–ligand structures can facilitate the low resistance transfer of electrons. Thus, $\mathsf{N i}_{3}\mathsf{H I T P}_{2}$ is classified as important candidates of electrocatalysts. However, the typical drawbacks of operation instability and high cost still limit the practical application of C-MOFs.
The use of 2D functional substrates as supporting materials is an advantageous strategy to overcome the typical drawbacks of $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}\mathrm{HITP}_{2}$ and has advantages of high stability, conductivity, and relatively low cost. Among all 2D substrates, reduced graphene oxide (rGO) has attracted most attention because of the abundant functional groups (e.g., hydroxyl) that can act as binding sites for $\mathsf{N i}_{3}\mathsf{H I T P}_{2}$ [6]. The excellent 2D structure of rGO can effectively improve the affinity of $\mathsf{N i}_{3}\mathsf{H I T P}_{2}$ , considerably augmenting the electron transfer and electrocatalytic activity of C-MOFs/rGO heterostructure [7]. As a result, the C-MOFs/rGO heterostructure with promising conductivity will be beneficial to the wide application of C-MOFs. According to theoretical calculations, the remarkable improvement in conductivity is also confirmed as described elsewhere [8,9]. Similar results were also obtained by experimental verification. Mu et al. [10] and Meng et al. [11] demonstrated the superior performance of 2D/2D C-MOFs/rGO due to the enhanced activity of metal center and improved electron harvesting and transfer between rGO and C-MOF. Although theoretical and experimental studies exhibited such favorable advantages of C-MOF/rGO heterostructure, the distinct mechanism of functional sites remains unexplored. Except metal sites and organic ligands, the electron transfer orientation between C-MOFs and rGO layers can also steer the activity of the C-MOFs/rGO heterostructure. This study emphasizes (1) the function of a separate metal site and dominant active species to be triggered, (2) the function of organic ligands especially N sites linked with metal atoms, and (3) the electron transfer orientation between C-MOFs and rGO layers to clarify such microscopic mechanism of catalytic reaction.
To uncover the abovementioned aspects, we take $\mathsf{N i}_{3}\mathrm{HITP}_{2}$ in CMOFs as research object, and $\mathrm{Ni_{3}H I T P_{2}/r G O}$ heterostructure was fabricated for application to the electrocatalytic oxidation of APAP for model demonstration. The function of separating metal sites and organic ligands was methodically determined by masking metal sites and replacing organic ligands, respectively. The function adjustment of metal sites and organic ligands was constructed, and the active species generation (including radicals and nonradicals) was identified. The electron transfer orientation between C-MOFs and rGO layers was analyzed by characterization and density functional theory (DFT) calculations. This innovative research elucidates the corresponding relationship between the active site and the generation of active species and discusses the mechanism of the synergistic interaction between $\mathsf{N i}_{3}\mathsf{H I T P}_{2}$ and rGO.
## 2. Experimental part
## 2.1. Chemicals and reagents
All chemicals and reagents used in this work, including their source and purity, were listed in Text S1 (Supplemental Material).
## 2.2. Catalyst synthesis
## 2.2.1. Synthesis of $N i_{3}H I T P_{2}$ and NiHAT-CN nanosheets
The 2D C-MOFs $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}\mathrm{HITP}_{2}$ and NiHAT-CN were prepared in accordance with previous reports [12]. First, 0.038 mmol HITP 6HCl or HAT-CN and 0.056 mmol $\mathsf{N i C l}_{2}{\cdot}6\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ were dissolved in $10~\mathrm{mL}$ distilled water. Second, $\mathsf{N i C l}_{2}$ solution was added dropwise into the HITP 6HCl or HAT-CN solution under magnetic stirring to form a well-proportioned solution, which was added with $600~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ ${\mathrm{NH}}_{3}{\cdot}{\mathrm{H}}_{2}0$ ( $\cdot14{\mathrm{~mol}}{\cdot}{\mathrm{L}}^{-1},$ afterward. Third, the hybrid solution was transferred into a Teflon-lined autoclave and reacted at $65~^{\circ}C$ for $^{3\mathrm{~h~}}$ . Finally, the suspension was centrifuged after the reaction, and the resulting black powder was washed with deionized water and acetone to remove unreacted metal ions and organic ligands several times. The product was dried at $60~^{\circ}C$ in a vacuum oven for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ .
## 2.2.2. Synthesis of graphene oxide nanosheets
Graphene oxide (GO) nanosheets were synthesized using the modified Hummers method [13]. First, $1\mathrm{~g~NaNO}_{3}$ , $_\mathrm{~1~g~}$ graphite powder, and $50~\mathrm{mL}$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ $(98\%)$ were mixed in a $250~\mathrm{mL}$ threeneck flask. The solution was stirred magnetically in an ice bath for $30\ \mathrm{min}$ until the temperature dropped below $5~^{\circ}C$ Second, the solution was slowly added with $6\textrm{g}\mathsf{K M n O}_{4}$ within $30~\mathrm{min}$ and added with $20~\mathrm{mL}$ distilled water by using a dropper into the above reaction system. When the temperature of the reacted solution reached $35^{\circ}\mathsf C\mathsf{-}40^{\circ}\mathsf C,$ it was maintained for $30~\mathrm{{min}}$ . Third, the solution was added with distilled water steadily until the reaction temperature reached $98\ ^{\circ}\mathsf C,$ incubated for $^\textrm{\scriptsize1h}$ cooled down under $35\ ^{\circ}\mathsf C$ , and added with $50~\mathrm{mL}$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ $(30\%)$ under stirring. Finally, the product was washed with HCl and ethanol $(C_{2}\mathrm{H}_{5}0\mathrm{H})$ with centrifugation to remove the sulfate and metal ions and then dissolved in $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{5}\mathrm{OH}$ . The product was subjected to ultrasound for $^{1\mathrm{~h~}}$ to acquire high-quality GO nanosheets.
## 2.2.3. Synthesis of $N i_{3}H I T P_{2}@r G O$
A simple physical mixture of $\mathsf{N i}_{3}\mathrm{HITP}_{2}$ and rGO was prepared to serve as a comparison material. About 5 mL GO solution was transferred into Teflon autoclave for hydrothermal treatment at $180^{\circ}{\mathsf C}$ for $^{3\mathrm{~h~}}$ . The product was washed, dried, and ground to obtain rGO powder. About $10\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{r}\mathrm{G}0$ and $30\mathrm{mg}\mathsf{N i}_{3}\mathrm{HITP}_{2}$ were dissolved with $20~\mathrm{mL}$ distilled water and stirred for $60\mathrm{min}$ to form a uniform suspension. The suspension was filtered and dried at $60^{\circ}\mathsf C$ overnight, and the resulting mixture was labeled $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}\mathrm{HITP}_{2}@\mathrm{r}\mathrm{G}0$ .
2.2.4. Synthesis of $N i_{3}H I T P_{2}/r G O$ and NiHAT-CN/rGO composite
Various amounts of $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}\mathrm{HITP}_{2}$ powder (10, 20, 30, and $40~\mathrm{mg}$ ) were dispersed in $50~\mathrm{mL}$ distilled water and subjected to ultrasound for $30\ \mathrm{min}$ . The solution was added with $1\ \mathrm{mL}$ GO (ca. $10~\mathrm{mg}{\cdot}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ ) under magnetic stirring and subjected to ultrasound for $90\mathrm{min}$ . The homogeneous mixture was formed and transferred into Teflon autoclave for hydrothermal treatment at $180^{\circ}{\mathsf{C}}$ for $^{3\mathrm{h}}$ . The product was washed with ethanol and water after reaction several times and then filtered through a filtration membrane under vacuum conditions. The mixture was dried overnight at $60~^{\circ}\mathsf C$ . The obtained $\mathrm{Ni_{3}H I T P_{2}/r G O}$ composite with various ratios was labeled $\mathrm{\sfNH}/\mathrm{\sfG}{-}x,$ where $\begin{array}{r l}{x}&{{}=~1}\end{array}$ , 2, 3, and 4, representing the added mass ratio of $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}\mathrm{HITP}_{2}$ to rGO. During the synthesis of NiHAT-CN/rGO, the added mass ratio of NiHAT-CN and GO was 3:1, and the other steps were consistent with the synthesis of $\mathsf{N i}_{3}$ - $\mathsf{H I T P}_{2}/\Gamma\mathsf{G O}$ . The resulting product was named NiHAT- $.{\mathsf{C N}}/{\mathsf{G}}$ .
## 2.3. Materials characterization
Transmission electron microscopy (TEM, JEOL-2100F) was used to investigate the morphological features of self-made catalysts. The representative groups on the surface of catalysts were detected via Fourier transform infrared (FTIR) spectroscopy (Nicolet iS50 spectrometer, Thermo Scientific). Raman spectrometry (Renishaw in Via) was carried out to analyze the defects of catalysts. X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS, Thermo Scientific Escalab 250Xi) was conducted to clarify the valence-state environment of elements and chemical bonds of the $\mathrm{Ni_{3}H I T P_{2}/r G O}$ composite. Electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) was executed at the electrochemical workstation (CHI660E, Chenhua). The electrolyte solution was $0.1\ \mathrm{mol}{\cdot}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ KOH, and results were fitted by the ZView software.
## 2.4. Experimental design and analysis method
The electrocatalytic degradation of $100~\mathrm{{mL}}$ APAP $(10\ \mathrm{mg}{\cdot}\mathrm{L}^{-1}.$ was conducted in a beaker by using a three-electrode system, and the electrolyte solution was $1\ {\mathrm{g}}{\cdot}{\mathrm{L}}^{-1}$ $\mathrm{\langle0.017~mol~L^{-1}}$ ) NaCl, the same molar concentrations of ${\mathsf{N a N O}}_{3}$ , ${\bf N a}_{2}{\bf C O}_{3}$ , and ${\tt N a}_{2}{\tt S O}_{4}$ were used for the electrolyte experiment. About $2\mathrm{~\mg~}\mathsf{N H}/\mathsf{G}{-}x$ was coated evenly onto carbon cloth $(2\ \mathrm{cm}\ \times\ 2\ \mathrm{cm})$ with electrically conductive adhesive. The carbon cloth was fixed using an electrode holder, which was used as anode after drying. The copper sheet was used as cathode, and the distance of two electrodes was $1\mathrm{cm}$ apart. Furthermore, the $\mathsf{A g/A g C l}$ electrode served as the reference electrode, and a PS-12 constant current rectifier (OuYa Zhongxing Technology) was used to supply the direct current constantly. During the active-site exploration, $2\mathrm{\mg\NH/G{-}}3$ -coated anode was pretreated by soaking the anode with $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ $(98\%)$ or $0.1\mathrm{\mol{cdot}L^{-1}\ N a}_{2}S$ for $45\mathrm{min}$ and then cleaning the surface with distilled water. After drying, the APAP degradation experiment was conducted. Before the quenching experiment, $10\mathrm{mmol}{\cdot}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ phenol, L-histidine, BQ, and TBA were dissolved in the reaction solution as quenching agents. During the degradation experiment of APAP, $1\ \mathrm{mL}$ sample was obtained using a pipette at regular intervals and added with 1 mL MeOH to capture excrescent radicals. Samples were filtered via $0.22\mathrm{~mm}$ filter paper before conducting high-performance liquid chromatography (HPLC, Waters ACQUITY Arc), which was equipped with a 2998PDA detector and C18 column (Waters CORTECS). The mobile phase was composed of ultrapure water and MeOH (28:72, v/v), the constant flow rate was $0.8\ \mathrm{mL}{\cdot}\mathrm{min}^{-1}$ , the detector wavelength was $246~\mathrm{{nm}}$ , and the column temperature was $35~^{\circ}C$ . The removal efficiency of APAP was expressed by $1\mathrm{~-~}\mathrm{C_{i}/C_{o}}$ , where $\mathsf{C}_{\mathrm{i}}$ was the concentration of APAP at the designated time point, and $C_{\phantom{}_{0}}$ was the initial concentration.
All experiments were repeated twice. Take the average value as the analysis value, the error bars were the difference between the experimental value and the average value.
Electron paramagnetic resonance (EPR, Bruker model EMX$10/12)$ was performed to clarify the effective active species. About $10\ \mathrm{mmol}{\cdot}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ 5,5-dimethylpyrroline N-oxide (DMPO) and 2,2,6,6-t etramethylpiperidinyloxy (TEMPO) were added into $20~\mathrm{mL}$ water samples as spin-trapping reagents to trap the signals of radicals in the solution. N,N-diethyl-1,4-phenylenediamine spectrophotometry was performed to figure out the concentration of active chlorine produced at different reaction times. The quantitative analysis of cumulative OH was performed as follows. About $10\ \mathrm{mmol}{\cdot}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ BA was added into the reaction solution in advance, and the OH produced during the reaction reacted with BA and formed $p$ -HBA. During the reaction, $1~\mathrm{mL}$ water sample was obtained every $15~\mathrm{min}$ and immediately mixed with 1 mL MeOH to eliminate the influence of superfluous OH. After filtering through $0.22~\mathrm{mm}$ filter paper, the concentration of $p$ -HBA in the sample was determined by HPLC (Waters ACQUITY Arc). The composition of mobile phase was aqueous trifluoroacetic acid solution $(0.1\%)$ and acetonitrile $(65{:}35,\mathsf{v}/\mathsf{v})$ , the flow rate was $1\ \mathrm{mL}\cdot\mathrm{min}^{-1}$ , the detector wavelength was $255\mathrm{nm}$ , and the column temperature was $25\ ^{\circ}\mathsf C$ . The detection limit of $p$ -HBA by this method was $0.1~\upmu\mathrm{mol}{\cdot}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ , and the cumulative concentration of OH was 5.87 times that of $p$ -HBA [14].
The detection of CODcr in reaction solution was carried out in accordance with the standard method (potassium dichromate titration method) [15]. Two parallel determinations were performed, and took the average value was the result value. The leaching concentration of metal ions in the catalyst during the electrocatalytic reaction was measured by atomic absorption spectrometry (Shimadzu, AA-6880). The pretreatment process was as follows. The solution was centrifuged after the catalytic reaction, and the supernatant was collected and filtered using a $0.22~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ membrane. About $5\mathrm{mL}$ filtered supernatant was used for digestion by nitric acid $\mathrm{(HNO}_{3}\mathrm{)}$ . The digested solution was diluted 1000 times with $3\%\mathsf{H N O}_{3}$ . Three parallel determinations were performed, and took the average value was the result value. The intermediates produced during the degradation process were clarified by ultraperformance LC quadrupole time-of-flight mass spectrometry (MS, Agilent 1290-6545), which was performed on the ZORBAX Eclipse Plus C18 column with the mobile phase consisting of $0.1\%$ MeOH and ultrapure water $(20{:}80,\upnu/\upnu)$ . Negative electrospray ionization mode was carried out within a certain range of the charge/mass ratio $(m/z)$ . The range of MS analysis was 50–800.
Escherichia coli $\tt D H5a$ (obtained from Tsingke Biotechnology Co., Ltd.) was selected as a model microorganism to evaluate the biological toxicity of APAP and degradation intermediates. About $100~\mathrm{\mL}$ Luria–Bertani (LB) culture media were prepared in $250~\mathrm{mL}$ conical flask and added with $1~\mathrm{mL}$ reaction solutions collected at 0, 60, and $120\mathrm{min}$ . Culture medium added with distilled water was used as control group. Then, all culture media were sterilized at $120^{\circ}{\mathsf{C}}$ for $60\mathrm{{min}}$ . E. coli $\tt D H5a$ was inoculated under aseptic conditions and grown at $37\ ^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $^\textrm{\scriptsize4h}$ . Finally, the optical density (OD) of E. coli $\tt D H5a$ was measured at $600\ \mathrm{nm}$ with a UV–Vis spectrophotometer [16], and results were expressed as OD600.
## 2.5. Calculation details of DFT
The structural optimization and electronic structure were determined by carrying out the DMol3 code [17]. The generalized gradient approximation with the Perdew–Burke–Ernzerhof function [18] and all-electron double-numerical basis set with a polarized function (double numeric with polarization) was used in the calculation. The maximum displacement, convergence tolerance of energy, and maximum force were $5.0\times10^{-3}$ , $\bar{1}.0\times10^{-5}$ , and $2.0\times10^{-3}~\mathrm{Ha}.\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{-1}$ , respectively, $(1\mathrm{Ha}=27.21\mathrm{eV})$ for geometry optimization. All calculations considered the Grimme method for DFT-D correction. Each atom in the storage models was allowed to relax to the minimum in the enthalpy without any constraints.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Characterization of materials
The morphology of $\mathrm{Ni_{3}H I T P_{2}/r G O}$ composite was explored via TEM. As illustrated in Fig. 1a-b, rGO and $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}\mathrm{HITP}_{2}$ showed lamellar structures. Compared with those of rGO and $\mathsf{N i}_{3}\mathsf{H I T P}_{2}$ , the typical stacking layered sheets of $\mathsf{N H}/\mathsf{G}{-}3$ were observed in Fig. 1c-d. This phenomenon fully confirmed that $\mathsf{N i}_{3}\mathsf{H I T P}_{2}$ nanosheets were loaded uniformly with rGO. The small-sized $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}\mathrm{HITP}_{2}$ nanosheets were possibly well distributed in the interlayers of rGO to form the heterogeneous 2D/2D composite [10], as indicated in the arrows in Fig. 1d. Moreover, no significant crimp or distortion could be observed in Fig. 1c-d. In the $\mathrm{Ni_{3}H I T P_{2}/r G O}$ heterostructure, the maintained integrity of $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}\mathrm{HITP}_{2}$ and rGO sheet was important for preserving the separated activity of rGO and $\mathsf{N i}_{3}\mathsf{H I T P}_{2}$ .
The crystal defects of $\mathsf{N H}/\mathsf{G}{-}x$ were assessed via Raman spectroscopy to further confirm the combination of rGO and $\mathsf{N i}_{3}\mathrm{HITP}_{2}$ (Fig. 2a). All samples exhibited two typical peaks at 1353 (D band) and 1585 (G band) $\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ . The D band corresponded to the edge, disordered, and defective carbon ring. The G band was associated with the $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{E}}_{2\mathbf{g}}$ mode vibration of the $\mathsf{s p}^{2}$ hybrid carbon in the complete six-membered carbon ring structure [19,20]. The ratio of peak D to peak G $\left(\mathrm{I_{D}}/\mathrm{I_{G}}\right)$ presented the degree of the disordered structure. Results revealed that the $\mathrm{I_{D}/I_{G}}$ value of $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}\mathrm{HITP}_{2}$ was lower than that of rGO, directly indicating more ordered structure of $\mathsf{N i}_{3}\mathsf{H I T P}_{2}$ than that of rGO. This ordered structure resulted from the coordination of Ni with N in $\mathsf{N i}_{3}\mathsf{H I T P}_{2}$ , forming $\pi{-}\mathrm{d}$ conjugated 2D structure [21]. Notably, the structure of $\mathsf{N H}/\mathsf{G}{-}x$ $(x\ =\ 1{-}4)$ became more ordered in comparison with that of rGO. With the insertion of increased $\mathsf{N i}_{3}\mathsf{H I T P}_{2}$ nanosheets into rGO, a disordered structure of $\mathsf{N H}/\mathsf{G}-x\left(x=1-3\right)$ was obtained [10]. These results confirmed the possible bonding connection between rGO and $\mathsf{N i}_{3}\mathsf{H I T P}_{2}$ rather than simple mixing. However, when the added $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}\mathrm{HITP}_{2}$ content increased to $40\mathrm{mg}$ , the $\mathrm{I_{D}/I_{G}}$ value decreased, which might be due to the stacking of rGO and $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}\mathrm{HITP}_{2}$ layers.
FTIR spectroscopy was performed to explore the possible connection between rGO and $\mathsf{N i}_{3}\mathsf{H I T P}_{2}$ . Results were presented in Fig. 2b. In the spectrogram of $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}\mathrm{HITP}_{2}$ , the broad peak at $3431~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ was caused by the $-\mathsf{O H}$ bond because water was adsorbed on the surface of materials [22]. The peaks at 1620, 1522, and $1429~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ corresponded to the skeleton vibration of the benzene ring. Typically, peaks located at 1332 and $870~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ were related to the stretching vibration of $\mathsf{C-N}$ and in-plane flexural vibration of $\mathsf{N{\mathrm{-}}H}$ in the ligand, respectively [23]. In the spectrograms of rGO, two small absorption peaks at around $2900\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ were caused by CAH [24], and the characteristic peak at $1385\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ was caused by the C-OH bond. With the combination of $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}\mathrm{HITP}_{2}$ and rGO, the representative peaks of $\mathsf{N i}_{3}\mathsf{H I T P}_{2}$ (e.g., $870~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ ) and rGO (e.g., $1385~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ ) coexisted in the spectrum of $\mathrm{\sfNH}/\mathrm{\sfG}{-}x,$ indicating that $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}\mathrm{HITP}_{2}$ was successfully combined with rGO, and the heterogeneous structure was formed simultaneously. The peak of $\mathsf{C}{\mathop{=}}\mathsf{C}$ bond (benzene ring) in $\mathsf{N i}_{3}\mathsf{H I T P}_{2}$ showed a dramatic blueshift from $1620~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ to $1580~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . Simultaneously, the COH peak at $1385~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ in rGO exhibited a redshift to $1405~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ with increased $\mathsf{N i}_{3}\mathsf{H I T P}_{2}$ . This phenomenon was possibly caused by the $\pi{-}\pi$ interaction between rGO and $\mathsf{N i}_{3}\mathsf{H I T P}_{2}$ nanosheets. In addition, the CAN peak $(1332\thinspace{\mathrm{cm}}^{-1}).$ ) in $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}\mathrm{HITP}_{2}$ was totally diminished after combination with rGO, suggesting that new bonds were possibly generated at this position. This phenomenon likely led to the weak response of $\mathsf{C}{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{N}$ bond.
The composition and specific elemental electronic states of materials were further investigated via XPS. The survey spectra (Fig. 3a) revealed the existence of C, N, O, and Ni in $\mathsf{N i}_{3}\mathsf{H I T P}_{2}$ and $\mathsf{N H}/\mathsf{G}{-}3$ . Compared with that of $\mathsf{N i}_{3}\mathsf{H I T P}_{2}$ , the O content of $\mathsf{N H}/\mathsf{G}-$ 3 was slightly lower due to the exfoliation of oxygen-containing groups during hydrothermal progress. The contents of Ni and N in $\mathsf{N i}_{3}\mathrm{HITP}_{2}$ were close to the theoretical atom content [25], clearly presenting the integrated structure of self-made $\mathsf{N i}_{3}\mathsf{H I T P}_{2}$ . These results were consistent with those of TEM and Raman characterization. The spectra of O1s was shown in Fig. 3b. For $\mathsf{N i}_{3}\mathsf{H I T P}_{2}$ , the peaks at binding energies of 531.54 and $530.76~\mathrm{eV}$ corresponded to $_{\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}}$ and Ni-OH, where O originated from oxygen adsorbed on the surface of $\mathsf{N i}_{3}\mathsf{H I T P}_{2}$ [22]. After the combination of $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}\mathrm{HITP}_{2}$ and rGO, the O element in $\mathsf{N H}/\mathsf{G}{-}3$ originated from rGO in the composites. Specifically, the peaks at binding energy of 533.5, 532.4, and $531.3\ \mathrm{eV}$ corresponded to the functional groups of $_{C-0-C}$ , $C{\mathrm{-}}0\mathsf{H}$ , and $\mathsf{C}{=}0/0{-}\mathsf{C}{=}0$ [26,27], respectively.
The spectra of Ni 2p, C 1s, and N1s were further inspected to clarify the bonding connection of $\mathsf{N i}_{3}\mathsf{H I T P}_{2}$ and $\mathsf{N H}/\mathsf{G}{-}3$ . Fig. 3c and d show the spectra of $\mathsf{N i~}2\mathsf{p}$ and N 1s. As shown in Fig. 3c, the peaks at 856 and $873{\mathrm{~eV}}$ in the Ni 2p spectrum corresponded to Ni $2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}$ and Ni $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ , respectively. In Fig. 3d, the peak at $399{\mathrm{~eV}}$ in the N1s spectrum belonged to $\mathsf{N i-N_{4}}$ . The existence of Ni $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ , Ni $2{\tt p}_{3/2}$ , and Ni-N peaks indicated that the Ni ion and N in the ligand formed a $\mathrm{Ni-N_{4}}$ coordinate structure [28,29]. However, the Ni $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ and Ni $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ peaks of $\mathsf{N H}/\mathsf{G}{-}3$ displayed a slight increase in the binding energy (about $0.3\ \mathrm{eV}$ ). The $\mathsf{N i-N_{4}}$ peak of $\mathsf{N H}/\mathsf{G}{-}3$ shifted to higher binding energy (increased $0.4~\mathrm{eV}$ compared with that of $\mathsf{N i}_{3}\mathsf{H I T P}_{2}$ . The upshift of Ni $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ , $\mathsf{N i}~2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ , and Ni-N peaks indicated that $\mathsf{N i}_{3}\mathsf{H I T P}_{2}$ sheets possibly acted as electron acceptor after combination with rGO [10]. The generation of new electron transfer pathways between $\mathsf{N i}_{3}\mathrm{HITP}_{2}$ and rGO facilitated the electron seizing of $\mathsf{N i}_{3}\mathrm{HITP}_{2}$ sheets, leading to electronic state changes in Ni and N atom [10]. Moreover, dramatic changes in the $\mathsf{N i-N_{4}/N H_{2}\mathrm{-C_{6}H_{4}\mathrm{-NH_{2}}}}$ ratio and the upshift of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}–\mathrm{C}_{6}\mathrm{H}_{4}–$ $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ peaks in N 1s spectrum supported the same conclusion. The strong variation of the N 1s spectra also suggested the critical function of N sites, as revealed via FTIR analysis. In the spectra of C 1s (Fig. 3e), the peaks at 286.5, 284.8, and $283.8\ \mathrm{eV}$ corresponded to CAN, $\mathsf{C}{=}\mathsf{C}$ and $C{\mathrm{-}}C$ bonds, respectively, present in the $\mathsf{N i}_{3}\mathrm{HITP}_{2}$ nanosheet [30]. After binding with rGO, a new characteristic peak appeared in the spectra of C 1s, which corresponded to the $0{\cdot}C{=}0$ bond in rGO [31]. In addition, the peaks of the $C{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{N}$ and $\mathsf{C}{\mathop{=}}\mathsf{C}$ bonds shifted to low binding energy. The downshift of C 1s was caused by the electrostatic charge neutralization. This finding also implied partial electron transfer from the negatively charged rGO to the relatively positively charged $\mathsf{N i}_{3}\mathsf{H I T P}_{2}$ [10]. Abovementioned results supported the conclusion of N 1s spectrum and FTIR characterization. In summary, since the generation of new electron transfer pathways and electrostatic charge neutralization, negatively charged rGO could effectively adjust the electronic states of the $\mathsf{N i-N_{4}}$ moiety in $\mathsf{N i}_{3}\mathsf{H I T P}_{2}$ .
EIS is a powerful electrochemical characterization method that can be used to investigate the electrocatalytic processes occurring at the interface of solid electrodes and liquid electrolytes. Thus, EIS was implemented to assess the generation of new electron transfer pathways between $\mathsf{N i}_{3}\mathsf{H I T P}_{2}$ and rGO to explore the internal reasons for excellent catalytic activity. In the experiment and fitted results (Fig. 4 and Table 1), ${\sf R}_{1}$ represented the solution resistance from the reference electrode to the working electrode, ${\sf R}_{2}$ represented the charge-transfer resistance of the working electrodes,
CPE was a constant-phase element related to the double-layer capacity, and Wo was the generalized Warburg impedance $(Z_{\mathrm{w}})$ of the solid-phase diffusion [32]. As shown in Table 1, when the composite ratios of $\mathsf{N i}_{3}\mathrm{HITP}_{2}$ and rGO were 1:1, 2:1, and 3:1, $\mathsf{N H}/$ G showed dramatically lower ${\sf R}_{2}$ charge-transfer resistance of the working electrodes than pure $\mathsf{N i}_{3}\mathsf{H I T P}_{2}$ and rGO. This finding also confirmed the generation of new electron transfer pathways between $\mathsf{N i}_{3}\mathsf{H I T P}_{2}$ and rGO. In addition, ${\sf R}_{2}$ initially decreased with increased $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}\mathrm{HITP}_{2}$ content in $\mathsf{N H}/\mathsf{G}$ , suggesting that the charge transfer was promoted consequently [4,7]. When the mass ratio of $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}\mathrm{HITP}_{2}$ to rGO was 3:1, the ${\sf R}_{2}$ resistance reached the lowest value. Given that the embedding of extremely thick $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}\mathrm{HITP}_{2}$ layers disrupted the dispersion of rGO [10], the conductivity of $\mathsf{N H}/\mathsf{G}$ reduced as the $\mathsf{N i}_{3}\mathrm{HITP}_{2}$ content continually increased to 4:1. The resistance value of $\mathsf{N H}/\mathsf{G}{-}4$ was substantially higher than those of $\mathsf{N H}/\mathsf{G}{-}3$ and pure rGO due to the stacking of $\mathsf{N i}_{3}\mathrm{HITP}_{2}$ and rGO.
## 3.2. Electrochemical performance of $N i_{3}H I T P_{2}/r G O$ composite
The reaction efficiency of different catalysts was studied under applied current of $20~\mathrm{{mA}}$ , temperature of $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ , and $\mathsf{p H}=6.55$ to determine the synergy of combined functions of $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}\mathrm{HITP}_{2}$ and rGO. The physically mixed material $(\mathsf{N i}_{3}\mathsf{H I T P}_{2}@\mathsf{r G O}$ , with same mass ratio with $\mathsf{N H}/\mathsf{G}{-}3)$ was prepared for comparison to confirm the improvement of $\mathsf{N H}/\mathsf{G}{-}3$ for APAP degradation (Fig. 5a). The degradation efficiency of $\mathsf{N i}_{3}\mathrm{HITP}_{2}@\mathrm{r}\mathsf{G O}$ to APAP was between pure $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}\mathrm{HITP}_{2}$ and rGO, indicating the separate functions of $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}\mathrm{HITP}_{2}$ and rGO. Compared with that by $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}\mathrm{HITP}_{2}@\mathrm{r}\mathrm{G}0$ , the electrocatalytic degradation efficiency of APAP by NH/G-3 increased by about $10\%$ , indicating that the formation of the electron transfer bridge between $\mathsf{N i}_{3}\mathrm{HITP}_{2}$ and rGO promoted the degradation of APAP.
Furthermore, the input amounts of $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}\mathrm{HITP}_{2}$ and rGO were regulated to seek the optimal composite ratio. As shown in Fig. 5b, after the $\mathsf{N i}_{3}\mathsf{H I T P}_{2}$ was combined with rGO, the degradation efficiency of $\mathsf{N H}/\mathsf{G}$ was apparently better than that of $\mathsf{N i}_{3}\mathrm{HITP}_{2}$ or rGO. Furthermore, the reaction process accelerated with increased $\mathsf{N i}_{3}\mathsf{H I T P}_{2}$ . For $\mathrm{NH}/\mathrm{G}{-}1,\mathrm{NH}/\mathrm{G}{-}2,$ and $\mathsf{N H}/\mathsf{G}{-}3$ , a $1.9\%\pm2.69\%$ difference was achieved by adjusting the content of $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}\mathrm{HITP}_{2}$ due to the modification of the electron transfer bridge between $\mathsf{N i}_{3}\mathrm{HITP}_{2}$ and rGO. Results exhibited that the combination of $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}\mathrm{HITP}_{2}$ and rGO was beneficial to improve the catalytic activity via the enhanced active center and increased electron transfer. Experimental results were consistent with the findings of Raman spectroscopy and EIS, and $\mathsf{N H}/\mathsf{G}{-}3$ was the optimal material for subsequent experiments. As the content of $\mathsf{N i}_{3}\mathrm{HITP}_{2}$ increased to $40\mathrm{mg}$ , the degradation efficiency tended to be attenuated. For this phenomenon, excessive $\mathsf{N i}_{3}\mathsf{H I T P}_{2}$ accumulated to cover the active surface of the catalysts, leading to the exploitation of slight active centers in $\mathsf{N H}/\mathsf{G}{-}4$ to catalyze the oxidation reaction. This finding might also be due to the low transfer efficiency of electrons, resulting from the covering of excessively thick $\mathsf{N i}_{3}\mathsf{H I T P}_{2}$ layer onto the rGO layer [10].
APAP degradation and ion leaching were analyzed at various pH conditions to grasp further insight into the application possibility of $\mathsf{N H}/\mathsf{G}{-}3$ . As shown in Fig. 5c, NH/G-3 presented excellent efficiency in APAP degradation at the initial $\mathsf{p H}$ range from 3 to 9. When $\mathsf{p H}$ increased from 3.00 to 6.55, the removal efficiency of APAP decreased, indicating that the acidic environment could considerably promote APAP degradation. The generation of active species at various pH levels was possibly responsible for this phenomenon. The $\mathsf{C l^{-}}$ in the electrolyte would generate active chlorine through Eqs. (1–4). As a nonradical, active chlorine predominantly included $\mathsf{C l}_{2}$ , HClO, and $\mathsf{C l O}_{3}^{-}$ in this electrocatalytic system. The active chlorine primarily existed in strong HClO (oxidation potential of 1.47 V) form at acidic conditions [33], stimulating the degradation of APAP. In addition, OH was easily formed under acidic conditions. However, in alkaline conditions, OH reacted with $\mathsf{O H}^{-}$ to generate low oxidation potential O [34]. Even with the difference of efficiency obtained under pH 3–9, NH/G-3 still possessed excellent stability (efficiency $>93.06\%\pm1.15\%)$ in a wide pH range. Here, metal leaching, as index of stability of catalysts, was also considered. At $\mathsf{p H}3$ , the Ni ion leaching was shown in Fig. 5c. Evidently, the Ni leaching in $\mathsf{N H}/\mathsf{G}{-}3$ was significantly lower than that in pure $\mathsf{N i}_{3}\mathsf{H I T P}_{2}$ . Thus, combining $\mathsf{N i}_{3}\mathsf{H I T P}_{2}$ with rGO brought the benefits of lower metal leaching and strongly enhanced catalytic activity. These results clearly suggested promising environmental and economic efficiency.
