For further feasibility for application, this system was also estimated under various operation conditions. The electrocatalytic performance of the electrocatalytic oxidation technology was inevitably affected by the applied current and electrolyte. Fig. S1 shows the effect of applied current on the APAP degradation with $\mathsf{N H}/\mathsf{G}{-}3.$ . Degradation efficiency was only $74.73\%\pm\ 7.69\%$ when the applied current was $10\ \mathrm{mA}$ When the current increased to $20~\mathrm{{mA}}$ , the APAP removal efficiency reached $100.00\%\pm0.00\%$ due to abundant active species generated during the reaction [35]. However, as the applied current increased to 25 and $30~\mathrm{mA}$ , the degradation efficiency of APAP declined instead. This result pointed out that the side reaction (e.g., oxygen evolution reaction) on the working electrode was intensified [36,37]. Thus, the current of $20\mathrm{mA}$ was selected for further experiments. As shown in Fig. S2, the effect of electrolytes (i.e., ${\mathsf{N a N O}}_{3}$ , ${\bf N a}_{2}{\bf C O}_{3}$ , ${\ N a}_{2}S O_{4}.$ , and NaCl) on the oxidation of APAP was studied, and results showed that the degradation efficiency of APAP under these four electrolytes were $16.31\%\pm0.83\ \%,$ , $27.77\%\pm0.49\%$ , $42.12\%\pm3.58\%$ , and $100.00\%\pm$ $0.00\%$ , respectively. The removal of APAP was optimum when NaCl was used as electrolyte. The $\mathbf{Cl}^{-}$ in the solution could be oxidized to produce highly active chlorine species, including $\mathsf{C l}_{2}$ , HClO, and ${\mathsf{C l O}}_{3}^{-}$ (Eqs. 1–4), where HClO showed high reactivity toward the aromatic rings owing to the pronounced ionic nature [19]. When ${\mathsf{N a N O}}_{3}$ , ${\bf N a}_{2}{\bf C O}_{3}$ , and ${\tt N a}_{2}{\tt S O}_{4}$ were used as electrolytes, the degradation of APAP relied on direct oxidation [24], and indirect oxidation contributed minimally. In addition, $\mathsf{C O}_{3}^{2-}$ and $S0_{4}^{2-}$ were likely to quench OH because of the high affinity toward OH (Eqs. (5) and (6)) [19]. On the basis of the above analysis, NaCl was selected as electrolyte for other experiments.
