Fig. S3 presents the effect of temperature on the electrocatalysis oxidation of APAP. As the reaction temperature increased, the degradation efficiency decreased due to the different active chlorine species generated under different temperatures. When NaCl was used as electrolyte, the active chlorine produced in the system was one of the main functional substances for electrocatalysis [38]. The production pathway of active chlorine occurred primarily through reactions (1–4). First, the electrochemical oxidation of the chloride ion $(\mathsf{C l}^{-})$ produced $\mathsf{C l}_{2}$ (Eq. (1)), and $\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ was hydrolyzed into hypochlorous acid (HClO) and perchloric acid ${\mathrm{(HClO}}_{3}{\mathrm{)}}$ 1 through Equations (2–4). The electrode potentials of the three active chlorine substances were different and followed the order: $\Sigma{1}0_{3}^{-}\ (1.39\mathrm{~V})<\mathrm{HCl}0\ (1.47\mathrm{~V})<\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ (aq.) (1.63 V) [39]. $\mathsf{C l}_{2}$ (aq.) and HClO could significantly promote the degradation of APAP, whereas ${\mathrm{HClO}}_{3}$ had a relatively weak effect. As temperature increased, $\mathsf{C l}_{2}$ was mostly released in gaseous form, and the content of ${\mathrm{HClO}}_{3}$ in the solution increased. Thus, the reaction rate was reduced [40].
Durability is an important index to assess the effectiveness of the catalysts. Cyclic stability experiments were performed under the conditions of $\mathrm{I}=20~\mathrm{mA}$ , $\mathrm{T}=25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ , and $\mathsf{p H}=6.55$ to figure out the reusability of $\mathsf{N H}/\mathsf{G}{-}3$ . Results were shown in Fig. S4a, decreasing followed slight increasing tendency was observed within 4 cycles. In cycle 2, $92.69\%\pm0.63\%$ removal of APAP was achieved. However, $3-7\%$ improvement was obtained in cycles $3-$ 4. To verify the fluctuation of efficiency, the contribution of adsorption and degradation of each cycle were separated. The adsorption experiments of materials were performed before each cycle experiment to explore the reason for the slight difference between each cycle. As shown in Fig. S4b, before the first experiment, the catalyst had almost no adsorption capacity for pollutants, and the removal efficiency benefitted from the electrocatalytic oxidation of APAP. In cycle 2, no evident adsorption was observed (Fig. S4b). Interestingly, $3\%-7\%$ improvement of adsorption was obtained in the cycles 3–4 compared with that in cycle 2. This phenomenon indicated the slight difference between each cycle could be attributed to adsorption, the final efficiency of cycles 3–4 was attributed to adsorption and degradation. Even with the increased contribution of adsorption in cycles 3–4, the effect of electrocatalytic oxidation was still taken the greater contribution for APAP removal than adsorption. The increased adsorption capability of cycled materials was possible due to the changes in surface morphology and functional groups in $\mathsf{N H}/\mathsf{G}{-}3$ .
Then, the materials before each cycle experiment were characterized by TEM and FTIR respectively. As shown in Fig. S4c-f, compared with $\mathsf{N H}/\mathsf{G}{-}3$ in the first experiment, the TEM image of $\mathsf{N H}/\mathsf{G}{-}3$ in cycle 2 still maintained the typical layered stacking morphology, indicating the slight decrease of removal efficiency was caused by the blockage of byproducts [24]. As the number of cycles increased to 4, the consumption of active metal sites leading the broken of Ni-N coordination bond and lamellar structure of $\mathsf{N i}_{3}\mathsf{H I T P}_{2}$ . Heterogeneous and dispersed $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}\mathrm{HITP}_{2}$ structures were formed on the surface of rGO, and the cracks were favorable for the adsorption of APAP and byproducts. Furthermore, the adsorption effect was also confirmed by FTIR spectroscopy. As shown in Fig. S4g, NH/G-3 in cycle 2 possessed similar FTIR spectrogram with fresh $\mathsf{N H}/\mathsf{G}{-}3$ . The remained chemical structure of $\mathsf{N H}/\mathsf{G}{-}3$ in cycle 2 was consistent with TEM images. The intensity of the double peaks at $2920~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $2855~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (corresponding to CAH) were enhanced, which was caused by the blockage of byproducts. With the increase of cycle times, the $\mathsf{C}{\mathop{=}}\mathsf{C}$ bond of benzene ring $(1620~\mathrm{cm}^{-1})$ ) and $-\mathtt{C O-N H}$ bond [19] $(1403~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ ) from APAP and byproducts was gradually enhanced. This phenomenon also proved the adsorption of APAP and byproducts. In addition, the appearance of Ni-O bond [52] $(697~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , from changing of Ni-N coordination bond) indicated the destruction of $\mathrm{\DeltaNH/G}{-3}$ which in according with TEM images.
## 3.3. Mechanism discussion
## 3.3.1. Analysis of active species
The analysis of active species in the reaction has an essential effect on inferring the degradation mechanism of the catalytic process. In this research, active species was investigated by quenching experiment and EPR analysis. Puny polar phenol was used to cover the active sites on the surface to determine the role of active sites on $\mathsf{N H}/\mathsf{G}{-}3$ surfaces. As shown in Fig. 6a, APAP degradation was strongly inhibited in the presence of phenol in the solution, proving that the surface sites of $\mathsf{N H}/\mathsf{G}{-}3$ were crucial to the production of active radicals. TBA was used as OH quenching agent $\mathrm{\k}^{\prime}\mathbf{k}=4.8-7$ $6\times10^{8}\mathrm{M}^{-1}s^{-1}$ [41]). As shown of Fig. 6a, TBA inhibited the degradation efficiency of APAP by $9.87\%\pm0.19\%$ , suggesting that OH was one of the active radicals involved in the reaction. BQ and Lhistidine, as capturer of $0_{2}^{\bullet-}$ and ${}^{1}0_{2}$ , respectively, seriously inhibited the catalytic reaction, indicating that $0_{2}^{\bullet-}$ and $^10_{2}$ were the main active species in the degradation process. EPR analysis was used to confirm quenching results (Fig. 6b-c). DMPO was used as a spin-trapping reagent to detect OH and $0_{2}^{\bullet-}$ in the solution, whereas TEMPO was used as the spin-trapping reagent for $^10_{2}$ [42]. As shown in Fig. 6b, the representative sextet-peak of the ${\sf D M P O-O_{2}^{\bullet-}}$ could be observed, demonstrating the generation of $0\mathbf{\dot{\bar{2}}}^{-}$ in the reaction [43]. The absence of DMPO-HO signal was attributed to the low and nearly undetected OH produced in the system. Similarly, the typical triple peak of TEMPO- $^10_{2}$ [24] was observed in Fig. 6c. EPR analysis results were consistent with quenching experiment results, confirming that $0_{2}^{\bullet-}$ and ${}^{1}0_{2}$ were the main active species during degradation.
When NaCl was used as electrolyte, $\mathbf{Cl}^{-}$ could be converted into active chlorine $[\mathsf{C l}_{2}$ , HClO, and $\mathsf{C l O}_{3}^{-}$ ) in the solution, thus contributing to the nonradical degradation pathway of APAP. Therefore, active chlorine was detected to verify the active species in the reaction. In the presence or absence of APAP in the solution, the concentration of active chlorine produced in the system was detected over the course of the reaction (Fig. 6d). When only NaCl was present in the solution, the concentration of active chlorine produced in the system was significantly increased, indicating that the presence of APAP would consume abundant active chlorine and that active chlorine was one of the main active species to degrade APAP. In the first stage of the reaction, the concentration of APAP in the solution was higher than that of active chlorine, leading to the rapid consumption of the latter. Thus, the detected concentration of active chlorine was relatively low. As the reaction progressed, the concentration of pollutants in the system gradually decreased. That is, the production rate of active chlorine was higher than the consumption rate, resulting in a gradual increase in concentration. This finding confirmed that active chlorine was the primary active substance in the liquid phase of the degradation reaction.
## 3.3.2. Relationships between active species and active sites
Metal sites were diminished by washing with $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ or masking with ${\sf N a}_{2}{\sf S}$ to clarify the effect of Ni sites on the contributions of APAP degradation. Results were shown in Fig. 7a. When Ni sites were diminished by $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ , the APAP degradation efficiency of $\mathbf{Ni}_{3}$ - $\mathsf{H I T P}_{2}$ was consequently decreased. However, $\mathsf{N H}/\mathsf{G}{-}3$ showed stable performance and even slight improvement at the same situation. This phenomenon indicated that rGO could act as support material and significantly protect $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}\mathrm{HITP}_{2}$ against $\mathrm{H}_{2}S0_{4}$ etching. As minimal Ni active sites were consumed in $\mathrm{\DeltaNH/G{-}}3$ , a high and stable efficiency was maintained. Moreover, with combination of $\mathsf{N i}_{3}\mathsf{H I T P}_{2}$ and rGO, the slight loss of Ni sites did not destroy the complete structure of $\mathsf{N H}/\mathsf{G}{-}3$ . Instead, active defects possibly formed on the catalyst surface, leading to slight improvement in efficiency. With the strong shield of Ni sites via ${\mathsf{N a}}_{2}{\mathsf{S}}$ , the efficiency of $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}\mathrm{HITP}_{2}$ and $\mathsf{N H}/\mathsf{G}{-}3$ decreased. This phenomenon further confirmed the benefits of Ni site for electrocatalytic activity. In addition, the efficiency of $\mathsf{N H}/\mathsf{G}{-}3$ decreased more evidently than that of $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}\mathrm{HITP}_{2}$ after the pretreatment of ${\sf N a}_{2}{\sf S}$ . This result indicated that the function of Ni sites was dominant in $\mathsf{N H}/\mathsf{G}{-}3$ due to direct contributions as active sites and the positive function related to new charge transfer pathway generation, as indicated by EIS (Fig. 4). Despite the decrease in metal sites, the oxidative degradation efficiency of APAP remained about $80\%$ via $\mathsf{N H}/\mathsf{G}{-}3$ , indicating that organic ligands (in ${\mathrm{Ni}}_{3}{\mathrm{HITP}}_{2}{\mathrm{.}}$ ) and rGO in the material could also be used as active site of the reaction.
Prior quenching experiments showed that free radicals (e.g., OH, $^{1}0_{2},0_{2}^{\bullet-}$ , and $\bullet\mathsf{C l}$ ) were the main contributors to the oxidative degradation of pollutants. Therefore, the free radicals produced before and after ${\sf N a}_{2}{\sf S}$ treatment were experimentally compared to clarify the influence of metal sites on radical generation. Results were shown in Fig. 7b–e. As shown in Fig. 7b, OH could be generated with or without ${\sf N a}_{2}{\sf S}$ treatment, and only $4\%-16\%$ reduction was obtained after ${\sf N a}_{2}{\sf S}$ treatment. This phenomenon indicated that OH generation was not intensively related to Ni sites. The organic ligands (in ${\mathrm{Ni}}_{3}{\mathrm{HITP}}_{2}{\mathrm{.}}$ ) and rGO were possibly responsible for OH generation. After the supplement of APAP, the generated OH was rapidly consumed, confirming that OH was one of the active species in the degradation of APAP. As shown in Fig. 7c, when Ni sites were obscured in $\mathsf{N H}/\mathsf{G}{-}3$ , almost no characteristic peak of $0_{2}^{\bullet-}$ could be observed, suggesting the strong contribution of Ni site for the generation of $0_{2}^{\bullet-}$ . However, the loss of metal sites did not influence the characteristic peak intensity of ${}^{1}0_{2}$ , indicating that the Ni sites was not the generation sites of $^10_{2}$ . Similarly, a slight difference in the amount of active chlorine production was observed before and after ${\sf N a}_{2}{\sf S}$ treatment (Fig. 7e), implying that the Ni site was not the main site of producing active chlorine. Experimental results showed that Ni sites contributed to the generation of $0_{2}^{\bullet-}$ and partly OH. By contrast, rGO and organic ligands possibly contributed to the production of OH, ${}^{1}0_{2}$ , and active chlorine.
The ligands from HITP $\mathrm{{'}C_{18}H_{18}N_{6}}$ , molecular structure shown in Fig. S6a) to HAT-CN $\mathsf{C}_{18}\mathsf{N}_{12}$ , molecular structure shown in Fig. S6b) were implemented to further determine the function of organic ligands. The new nitrogen-rich HAT-CN ligand provided more N sites that could be combined with rGO compared with HITP. Compared with $\mathsf{N i}_{3}\mathsf{H I T P}_{2}$ , pure NiHAT-CN showed a slightly lower degradation efficiency of APAP, indicating that N sites were not direct active sites for reaction (Fig. 7f). However, the catalytic efficiency of NiHAT- $.C\mathrm{N}/\mathrm{r}G0$ was better than that of $\mathrm{Ni_{3}H I T P_{2}/r G O}$ . For this improvement, EIS analysis of NiHAT-CN/rGO was also applied in Fig. 4. The high interaction and electron transfer between
NiHAT-CN and rGO (via the N sites) might be the reasons for efficiency enhancement. This phenomenon proved the anchoring function of N sites.
In summary, the main conclusions were as follows. (1) Ni sites were capable of generating $0_{2}^{\bullet-}$ and partly OH. (2) Organic ligands (in $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}\mathrm{HITP}_{2};$ and rGO demonstrated critical contribution in OH, ${}^{1}0_{2}$ , and active chlorine generation. (3) N sites in $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}\mathrm{HITP}_{2}$ were not direct active sites for reaction, but the main anchoring sites accelerated the electron transfer between $\mathsf{N i}_{3}\mathsf{H I T P}_{2}$ and rGO layers.
## 3.3.3. Mechanism analysis by DFT
DFT calculations were used to determine the function of Ni sites and electron transfer orientation and further verify abovementioned experimental findings. The information of highest occupied molecular orbital (HOMO) and lowest unoccupied molecular orbital (LUMO) was obtained to confirm the electron donation and acceptance around the $\mathsf{N i}_{3}\mathsf{H I T P}_{2}$ and rGO layers (Fig. 8). As shown in Fig. 8a, a typical balanced HOMO intensity distribution could be observed in rGO. In a single layer of $\mathsf{N i}_{3}\mathsf{H I T P}_{2}$ in Fig. 8b, the highest HOMO intensity was located around the Ni position. After the combination of rGO and $\mathsf{N i}_{3}\mathsf{H I T P}_{2}$ in Fig. 8c, the electron donation (HOMO intensity) of the rGO layer capability was boosted. This phenomenon accorded with XPS and EIS findings and elucidated that the electron transfer occurred between $\mathsf{N i}_{3}\mathrm{HITP}_{2}$ and rGO (from rGO to ${\mathrm{Ni}}_{3}{\mathrm{HITP}}_{2}{\mathrm{.}}$ ) rather than separately in $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}\mathrm{HITP}_{2}$ and rGO layer.
Based on experimental results, the behavior of metal sites could be confirmed by DFT calculations. The model of $\mathrm{Ni_{3}H I T P_{2}/r G O}$ was settled as a unit of the two combined layers (Fig. 9a-b). Given that the charge density around metal sites and ligands (including C and
N sites) was related to the catalytic reactivity, the charge distribution on the overlapping and nonoverlapping parts of $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}\mathrm{HITP}_{2}$ and rGO was calculated. The metal Ni sites at the overlapping and nonoverlapping positions were marked as Ni1 and Ni2, respectively. Nearby C and N atoms were marked in Fig. 9c, and results were summarized in Table 2. The charge distribution analysis of nonoverlapping positions (including Ni2, N5-8, and C5-8) exhibited that Ni sites favored the charge supply, which evidently provided electrons to nearby N sites. Thus, N sites contained negative charges. As indicated in the literature, the relative positively charged sites were expected to be the main activity sites for redox reactions due to their strong capacity for electron withdrawal from organic pollutions [44,45]. These results agreed with experimental findings. Ni atoms were the preferential sites for electron withdrawal, leading to the generation of active species $(0_{2}^{\bullet-}$ and partly OH). The charge distribution on the overlapping Ni1 site and nearby N1-4 and C1-4 sites were also calculated. The charge density of the Ni1 site was relatively higher compared with that of the Ni2 site. The combination of $\mathsf{N i}_{3}\mathsf{H I T P}_{2}$ and rGO improved the activity of metal Ni site. Nearby N atoms considerably contributed to the Ni charge desperation and showed low negative charge density. With overlapping $\mathsf{N i}_{3}\mathsf{H I T P}_{2}$ and rGO, Ni1 sites still played a positive role in redox reactions, and this finding was consistent with the nonoverlapping $\mathsf{N i}_{3}\mathsf{H I T P}_{2}$ . Conductivity also provided valuable information about electrochemical activity. The unsaturated site was also used to assess the electron transfer capability of electrocatalysts. As shown in Fig. 9d, the d-orbit center of Ni atoms decreased from $-2.624~\mathrm{eV}$ (nonoverlapping position) to $-2.675\ \mathrm{eV}$ (overlapping position). This result indicated that the Ni1 sites were more likely acted as electron trap than the Ni2 site during redox reaction [46]. The activity of Ni sites was improved by combination with rGO due to enhanced oxidation capacity and increased electron transfer. DFT results above agreed with experimental results and implied the following. (1) The electron transfer was oriented from rGO to $\mathsf{N i}_{3}\mathsf{H I T P}_{2}$ . (2) The activity of metal Ni sites was improved after the combination of $\mathsf{N i}_{3}\mathsf{H I T P}_{2}$ and rGO, leading to high catalytic capacity of $\mathrm{Ni_{3}H I T P_{2}/r G O}$ heterostructure.
## 3.3.4. Speculation of degradation pathways
The CODcr detection of solutions at different reaction time points was performed to figure out the mineralization of APAP (Fig. 10a). When the reaction was carried out for $20\mathrm{min}$ , the CODcr value of the solution was not much different from the initial CODcr value. At progress 1 $\cdot0{-}20~\mathrm{\min})$ , APAP was predominantly degraded into macromolecular intermediates rather than ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ , and the overall organic content did not decrease significantly. Then, the CODcr value of the solution decreased significantly with reaction time (from 20 min to $60~\mathrm{{min}}$ ), and the removal efficiency of CODcr reached $70.20\%\pm0.71\%$ . At progress 2, the residual APAP and accumulated intermediates in the early stage were oxidized by effective active species into small molecular species, and ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ were generated. The CODcr measurement results indicated that $\mathsf{N H}/\mathsf{G}{-}3$ , as an electrocatalyst, could promote the generation of active species in the system and realize the mineralization of APAP.
A series of intermediate species was produced in the electrocatalytic degradation of organic compounds. The analysis of intermediates could infer the reaction routes and facilitate the toxicity assessment upon the reaction accessory substance. $\mathsf{N H}/\mathsf{G}{-}3$ was used as an electrocatalyst to interpret the degradation mechanism of APAP, and LC-MS was used to analyze the intermediate products qualitatively. Several converted products were listed in Table 3. The degradation pathways (Fig. 10b) of APAP were proposed accordingly. First, the active species attacked the branch chain of the benzene ring in APAP molecules, which was susceptible to breakage, and acetamide was produced. Subsequently, the $\mathsf{C-N}$ bond was broken, resulting in the production of acetaldehyde or monoethanolamine [47] and further decomposition into ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ . $\boldsymbol{-}\mathsf{N}\mathsf{H}_{2}$ transformed into $\mathsf{N O}_{3}^{-}$ through direct and indirect oxidations. Under the function of active chlorine, partial chloride ions replaced the hydrogen ions on benzene ring to form chlorinated aromatic intermediates [19]. Under the attack of oxygen active species ( OH, $0_{2}^{\bullet-}$ , and $^10_{2}$ ), 1,4-benzoquinone and hydroquinone were formed [48]. These chlorinated aromatic compounds, 1,4- benzoquinone and hydroquinone, were also noted to be electrochemically active electron shuttles dealing with persistent catalysis to complete degradation[49]. The -Cl and $^{\dag}=0$ groups on benzene ring could withdraw electrons from nearby sites, leading to the unbalanced distribution of electron density [50]. Subsequently, the negatively charged active group (e.g., $0_{2}^{\bullet-}$ ) was more favorable to attack by electron-deficient C atoms near the Cl- and $0=$ groups, forming a ring-opening product [51] and achieving dechlorination. Finally, all substances were broken into smallmolecule intermediates and further oxidized into ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ .
E. coli $\tt D H5a$ was used as a model microorganism to verify the biological toxicity of the solution after the reaction and meet the quality requirements of the water environment. In addition, the LD50 values (rat oral) of all intermediates according to Material Safety Data Sheet were listed in Table 3. As shown in Fig. 10c, compared with the blank control, the initial APAP solution had a certain inhibitory effect (about $44\%$ on the growth of E. coli $\tt D H5a$ . The reaction solution at $60\ \mathrm{min}$ inhibited the growth of E. coli $\tt D H5a$ by only about $20\%$ , and the toxicity of the solution was significantly reduced. At this time, a small amount of APAP and intermediate products with toxicity (e.g., 1,4-benzoquinone) were produced in the solution and had a certain effect on the growth of E. coli $\mathsf{D H}5\upalpha.$ As the reaction proceeded to $120\mathrm{min}$ , the reaction solution had almost no inhibitory effect on the growth of E. coli $\tt D H5a$ . At this time, the toxic organic compounds in the solution were basically completely decomposed into nontoxic small molecular organic compounds or ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ .
## 4. Conclusion
Serious active sites (including metal sites, organic ligands, and rGO basement) of C-MOFs/rGO were considered to always take part in the reaction simultaneously in parallel and/or series. However, previous research always simply focused on the improvement of conductivity, surface area, and efficiency [4,7,10,11,31]. Highly conductive Ni-MOF/rGO was prepared in this study and applied in the electrocatalytic oxidation of APAP to deepen the complicated mechanism of C-MOFs/rGO in electrocatalytic oxidation. The functions of metal sites, ligands, and rGO basement were separated and identified by quenching experiment, metal masking experiment, and DFT analysis. Key findings indicated that the significantly improved electrocatalytic performance of $\mathrm{N_{3}i H I T P_{2}/r G O}$ was attributed to three critical points. (1) Ni sites preferred to trap electrons, leading to the generation of free radicals ( OH and $0_{2}^{\bullet-}$ ) and promoting the radical pathway of APAP degradation. (2) Ligands and rGO basement could induce the production of active species (e.g., $^10_{2}$ , OH and active chlorine) and provide a nonradical pathway of APAP degradation. (3) Although not direct active sites, N sites acted as anchoring sites to enhance electron transfer, and the transfer orientation was from rGO to $\mathsf{N i}_{3}\mathsf{H I T P}_{2}$ . The significance of this study is the confirmation of the function of different active sites in C-MOFs/rGO composite during electrocatalytic oxidation of APAP, suggesting a basic guideline for effective APAP removal with practical values.
